y for example one of the  wards seems to have been devoted to  obstetrics  within 50 years of their founding the  knights hospitaler like the knights  templar had become a military order of  monk nights with a division of ranks  those who tended to the ill in the  hospital and those who fought on behalf  of the order  while the templars wore a white tunic  with a red cross the knights hospitaler  were easily identified by their black  tunics and white crosses  the last of the new orders of knighthood  to result from the crusades were the  teutonic knights this order was  established later than the templars and  the hospitals closer to the end of the  12th century but again like the other  two orders the impetus for their  existence came from a desire to help  those in need  in this case the teutonic knights set  themselves the task of caring  specifically for injured crusaders  the age of chivalry lasted from about  eleven hundred to fifteen hundred  and long before that however knights in  armor were becoming militarily  obsolete  chaucer's knight is already one of a  dying breed  it's estimated that in his day there  were no more than one thousand nights in  the whole of england supported by about  three times as many squires and that was  down from six thousand nights in the  reign of henry ii 11 54 to 1189  one of the factors that had brought  about a reduction in their ranks was a  phenomenon that derived from the other  side of history the common soldier  provided with nothing more sophisticated  than a longbow and a quiver full of  arrows  the first battle to be decided by the  longbow was cressi in 1346 which the  english won against the french  it's been estimated that in the first  five minutes of the battle the english  bowmen were able to unleash five  thousand arrows thereby creating havoc  in the ranks of french knights  the longbow used with great success by  the english in the hundred years war  even became a point of national pride  and the english boasted the skill and  training required to fire it effectively  in 1549 english bishop hugh latimer  proclaimed  men shall never shoot the longbow well  except that they be brought up in it  the pull of the longbow is 80 pounds  requiring noteworthy strength as well  heavily armored pikemen also filled the  king's ranks tightly packed into  formation  unlike our noble knight the role of the  pikemen was largely defensive and  required almost no training  these pikemen would place the butt of  their weapons into the ground sticking  out from the earth at a 45 degree angle  and then they would prop them on the  backs of the men in front of them  their ranks were virtually impenetrable  by enemy cavalry and infantry  by the early 16th century swiss spanish  german and italian armies had shortened  the pikes and developed complex  maneuvers to utilize pikemen offensively  these operations required more extensive  drilling to ensure that the troops moved  properly within their formations  south of the alps as we know feudalism  didn't feature prominently although  lordship did  during the italian renaissance courtiers  were expected to perform military  service as part of their wide skill set  devoted to the service of their princes  mercenaries were far more in evidence in  the south as republics prosecuted their  wars and mercenary captains condotieri  gained fame  wealth and even political authority for  their abilities on the battlefield  so now let's fast forward  the soldier of the mid 17th century was  a far different specimen than the  warriors we've just been tracking  no longer the loyal knight or courtier  of renaissance kings and princes nor the  older style mercenaries serving the  republics this later soldier was in many  ways a tool for the early modern state  he wasn't born to military pursuits but  instead found himself either joining  voluntarily in hopes of bettering his  personal condition or being forced to  enter service because of his meager  station in life or perhaps because a  criminal past limited his opportunities  even soldiers for hire weren't drawn  from the upper tiers of society or in  possession of any lengthy preparation  for a career in the military  our soldiers training was condensed he  didn't need substantial skills to use  his weapons  and most of his exercises involved  drilling to ensure precise movement in  formation with his fellow soldiers  as an individual our soldier wouldn't be  a particularly effective weapon  his success was instead dependent on his  unit as a whole  muskets and archibuses had replaced bows  and arrows and even a tripod was used to  steady the gun  still these firearms were inaccurate  a smooth bore and a round shot meant  that even perfectly aimed projectiles  took off and wildly unpredictable paths  taking the time to aim carefully and  then fire was largely a wasted effort so  training troops and how to use these  weapons was relatively limited nearly  anyone could fire these weapons with  virtually the same effect  officers were still drawn from the  highest social ranks but military  service had ceased to be the privilege  of the elites  hey thanks for watching if you'd like to  learn more about the topics in this  episode the full list of series that  these clips came from is in the  description below you can watch them all  on one dream and don't forget to  subscribe to this youtube channel for  new episodes of perspectives and you can  watch previous ones here
 

